{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" The three deployment eras\n\n**Traditional deployment era.** Early on, organizations ran applications on physical servers. There was no way to define resource boundaries for applications on a physical server, and this caused resource-allocation issues. If multiple applications run on one"}},"session_id":"ce98ddcf-ef6a-43ed-983e-86339f179dfc","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"3d2dcafe-8b93-464e-9d57-c0d3e3901835"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" physical server, one application can take up most of the resources, and the others underperform. The available solution was to run each application on a different physical server, which did not scale, because resources sat underutilized and maintaining many phys"}},"session_id":"ce98ddcf-ef6a-43ed-983e-86339f179dfc","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"8057c3e1-273c-4335-aaae-a8cabfc74b78"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ical servers was expensive [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23].\n\n**Virtualized deployment era.** Virtualization allowed multiple Virtual Machines to run on a single physical server's CPU. Applications became isolated between VMs, which provides a level"}},"session_id":"ce98ddcf-ef6a-43ed-983e-86339f179dfc","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"4defbbad-f9f6-47ca-b29d-ac4a83a1cd3f"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" of security because the information of one application cannot be freely accessed by another. Utilization improved, hardware costs dropped, and scalability improved because an application could be added or updated easily. With virtualization you can present a set of physical resources as a cluster of disposable virtual machines. But each VM"}},"session_id":"ce98ddcf-ef6a-43ed-983e-86339f179dfc","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"38fc3718-9223-4f8b-8f40-0e33bd9cb5db"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" is a full machine running all the components, including its own operating system, on top of virtualized hardware [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23].\n\n**Container deployment era.** Containers are similar to VMs, but they have"}},"session_id":"ce98ddcf-ef6a-43ed-983e-86339f179dfc","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"cf0497bd-5935-4110-8556-9c6299aa325f"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" relaxed isolation properties that let them share the operating system among the applications. That's why containers are considered lightweight. Like a VM, a container has its own filesystem, share of CPU, memory, process space, and more. Because they are decoup"}},"session_id":"ce98ddcf-ef6a-43ed-983e-86339f179dfc","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"8272520e-8f5d-4d9b-aade-63b3cd23075c"}
